import json, os
from pathlib import Path


records = [
("u3_001","What is Distributed Data Processing (DDP)?","Distributed Data Processing (DDP) is the division of data processing tasks among multiple computers that communicate through a network.","Distributed Data Processing is the division of data processing tasks among multiple computers that communicate through a network.",["0"],["e56a590b-e4cb-4078-87ef-d16fd0d2510d"],"Distributed Data Processing","easy","definition"),
("u3_002","How does the bank-branch example illustrate Distributed Data Processing?","Each branch maintains local customer records, the branches communicate through a central server, and transactions can be performed from any branch. This distributes data and processing across connected locations.","Consider a bank having branches in Mumbai, Pune, Delhi, and Chennai.\n\n- Each branch maintains local customer records.\n- Branches communicate with a central server.\n- Transactions can be performed from any branch.\n\nThis is distributed data processing.",["0"],["e56a590b-e4cb-4078-87ef-d16fd0d2510d"],"Distributed Data Processing","medium","application"),
("u3_003","State four advantages of Distributed Data Processing listed in the notes.","The listed advantages include faster processing, improved reliability, better resource utilization, scalability, and reduced communication cost.","## Advantages\n\n- Faster processing\n- Improved reliability\n- Better resource utilization\n- Scalability\n- Reduced communication cost.",["1"],["21bf5731-ace7-4710-be87-71d854598747"],"Distributed Data Processing","easy","list"),
("u3_004","What is a Distributed Database System (DDBS)?","A Distributed Database System is a collection of logically related databases distributed across multiple locations and connected through a communication network. To users, it appears as a single database.","A Distributed Database System (DDBS) is a collection of logically related databases distributed across multiple locations and connected through a communication network.\n\nTo users, the distributed database appears as a single database.",["1"],["21bf5731-ace7-4710-be87-71d854598747"],"Distributed Database System","easy","definition"),
("u3_005","What does it mean for a distributed database to appear as a single database to users?","Although its portions are stored at different physical locations, they are logically interconnected and presented to users as one database.","A distributed database is a database whose portions are stored at different physical locations but are logically interconnected.\n\nTo users, the distributed database appears as a single database.",["1"],["21bf5731-ace7-4710-be87-71d854598747"],"Distributed Database System","medium","conceptual"),
("u3_006","What is data sharing in a distributed database?","Data sharing means data stored at different locations can be shared and accessed by multiple users or branches at the same time.","Data stored at different locations can be shared and accessed by multiple users or branches at the same time.\n\nInstead of keeping separate copies of data at every branch, users can access the required data from the distributed database. This improves collaboration and avoids duplicate data.",["2"],["27c678dd-585c-4809-8659-47f950ac54e7"],"Data Sharing","easy","definition"),
("u3_007","Explain data sharing using the bank example in the notes.","A customer can open an account at the Mumbai branch and later visit the Pune branch, where the customer's account details can still be accessed. Users at different locations can access the same data.","A bank has branches in Mumbai, Pune, and Nagpur.\n\n- A customer opens an account in Mumbai.\n- Later, the customer visits the Pune branch.\n- The Pune branch can access the customer's account details even though they were created in Mumbai.\n\nThis is Data Sharing.\n\nUsers at different locations can access the same data.",["3"],["4e9da7d9-e36a-4526-ae22-bf6d812b1aab"],"Data Sharing","medium","application"),
("u3_008","What is Distribution Transparency?","Distribution Transparency means users do not need to know where data is physically stored; the database system automatically finds and retrieves it.","Users do not need to know where the data is physically stored. The database system automatically finds and retrieves it.\n\nEven if data is stored on different servers, users write the same SQL query. The Distributed DBMS decides where to find the data.",["3"],["4e9da7d9-e36a-4526-ae22-bf6d812b1aab"],"Distribution Transparency","easy","definition"),
("u3_009","How does the SELECT query example demonstrate Distribution Transparency?","A student runs `SELECT * FROM Student;` without knowing whether the data is stored in Delhi, Mumbai, or Hyderabad. The DBMS automatically retrieves the required data.","A student searches for:\n\nSELECT * FROM Student;\n\nThe student does not know whether the data is stored in Delhi, Mumbai, or Hyderabad. The DBMS automatically retrieves the required data.\n\nThis is Distribution Transparency.",["4"],["bf584d12-bcb1-48e0-9eef-15cb68fb4ab5"],"Distribution Transparency","medium","application"),
("u3_010","What is Local Autonomy in a distributed database?","Local Autonomy means each site has the freedom to manage and control its own local database, including operations such as inserting, updating, deleting, backing up data, and managing security.","Each site has the freedom to manage and control its own local database.\n\nEvery branch can perform its own operations such as:\n\n- Insert records\n- Update records\n- Delete records\n- Backup data\n- Manage security\n\nwithout depending on other branches.",["4"],["bf584d12-bcb1-48e0-9eef-15cb68fb4ab5"],"Local Autonomy","easy","definition"),
("u3_011","How does the university-campus example illustrate Local Autonomy?","The Pune campus can update its student records without asking permission from the Mumbai campus. Each campus manages its own data independently.","A university has campuses in:\n\n- Pune\n- Mumbai\n- Nashik\n\nThe Pune campus can update its student records without asking permission from the Mumbai campus.\n\nEach campus manages its own data independently.",["4","5"],["bf584d12-bcb1-48e0-9eef-15cb68fb4ab5","f2618c3e-fb79-4204-a658-10fc4f7fb21d"],"Local Autonomy","medium","application"),
("u3_012","What is Reliability in a distributed database?","Reliability means that if one site or server fails, the entire database system continues to work using the remaining sites.","If one site or server fails, the entire database system continues to work using the remaining sites.\n\nSince data is distributed (and sometimes replicated), failure at one location does not stop the whole system.",["5"],["f2618c3e-fb79-4204-a658-10fc4f7fb21d"],"Reliability","easy","definition"),
("u3_013","What is Scalability in a distributed database?","Scalability means the distributed database can grow by adding new sites or servers without major changes to the system.","The distributed database can grow easily by adding new sites or servers without major changes to the system.\n\nAs an organization expands, new branches or servers can be connected to the existing distributed database.",["6"],["bc007b91-27c8-452f-9205-0cd5840b1b20"],"Scalability","easy","definition"),
("u3_014","What is the purpose of Distributed Database System architecture?","It defines how data is stored, managed, and processed across multiple database sites connected through a communication network, and how sites communicate and users access distributed data as if it were one database.","The Architecture of a Distributed Database System (DDBS) defines how data is stored, managed, and processed across multiple database sites connected through a communication network. It specifies how different computers (sites) communicate with each other and how users access distributed data as if it were a single database.",["7"],["a0fcc2ff-4204-429a-b347-cc8b015a8998"],"DDBS Architecture","medium","definition"),
("u3_015","Name the three main architectures of Distributed Database Systems.","The three main architectures are Client-Server Architecture, Peer-to-Peer Architecture, and Multi-Database Architecture.","There are three main architectures of Distributed Database Systems:\n\n1. Client-Server Architecture\n2. Peer-to-Peer Architecture\n3. Multi-Database Architecture",["8"],["84ec2451-b0c9-4ad6-b6fa-ec6dc37baa5b"],"DDBS Architecture","easy","list"),
("u3_016","Describe how Client-Server Architecture works in a distributed database.","The client sends a database request, such as an SQL query, to the database server. The server receives and processes the query, retrieves or updates the required data, and returns the result to the client.","1. The client sends a database request (such as a SQL query).\n2. The database server receives the request.\n3. The server processes the query.\n4. The server retrieves or updates the required data.\n5. The server returns the result to the client.",["9"],["9259a296-d4f5-4a2b-a35a-3f4e778ac14f"],"Client-Server Architecture","medium","process"),
("u3_017","State two disadvantages of Client-Server Architecture.","The server can become a bottleneck when many clients access it simultaneously, and server failure can affect all connected clients because it is a single point of failure.","- Server can become a bottleneck if many clients access it simultaneously.\n- Failure of the server can affect all connected clients (single point of failure).",["10"],["2722a582-4816-41cd-b813-26f648c6f0c9"],"Client-Server Architecture","easy","list"),
("u3_018","What is Peer-to-Peer (P2P) Architecture?","In Peer-to-Peer Architecture, every site acts as both a client and a server. There is no central server; each site can request data from other sites and provide its own data.","In Peer-to-Peer Architecture, every site acts as both a client and a server. There is no central server. Each site can request data from other sites and also provide its own data.",["10"],["2722a582-4816-41cd-b813-26f648c6f0c9"],"Peer-to-Peer Architecture","easy","definition"),
("u3_019","What is Multi-Database Architecture?","Multi-Database Architecture consists of multiple independent (autonomous) databases that cooperate to provide users with integrated access to data. Each database may use a different DBMS and is managed independently.","A Multi-Database Architecture consists of multiple independent (autonomous) databases that cooperate to provide users with integrated access to data.\n\nEach database may use a different DBMS and is managed independently.",["11"],["32b9185d-45cd-4a7d-88d0-58b160d994d4"],"Multi-Database Architecture","easy","definition"),
("u3_020","Give the example of Multi-Database Architecture described in the notes.","A company uses Oracle for employee records, MySQL for customer information, and PostgreSQL for sales data. A report combines information from all three databases.","A company uses:\n\n- Oracle Database for employee records\n- MySQL Database for customer information\n- PostgreSQL Database for sales data\n\nA report combines information from all three databases.",["12"],["5372ec6d-b7fd-4611-85a8-e442a514d5e1"],"Multi-Database Architecture","medium","application"),
("u3_021","Differentiate homogeneous and heterogeneous distributed databases.","A homogeneous distributed database uses the same DBMS software at all sites. A heterogeneous distributed database uses different DBMS software at different sites.","All sites use the same DBMS software.\n\nDifferent sites use different DBMS software.\n\nMumbai → Oracle\n\nDelhi → SQL Server\n\nPune → MySQL",["13"],["185f7588-b831-40b3-b5be-6155d06be8de"],"Homogeneous vs Heterogeneous","medium","comparison"),
("u3_022","What is Data Distribution as a design issue in distributed databases?","Data Distribution means deciding at which site or location the data should be stored.","Data Distribution means deciding at which site or location the data should be stored.",["14"],["6b8134d8-69da-46e6-824d-a148f5f3df70"],"Data Distribution","easy","definition"),
("u3_023","What is Fragmentation in a distributed database?","Fragmentation is the process of dividing a database or relation into smaller logical parts called fragments, which can be stored at different sites.","Fragmentation means dividing a large database into smaller parts called fragments.\n\nFragmentation is the process of dividing a relation (table) into smaller logical parts called fragments, which can be stored at different locations/sites in a distributed database.",["15"],["c62da33e-3f19-4988-b9ba-46a702f49223"],"Fragmentation","easy","definition"),
("u3_024","Why is fragmentation used in distributed databases?","Fragmentation places data closer to the users or applications that frequently access it. The notes list improved query performance, reduced communication cost, support for parallel processing, improved availability, improved scalability, and better data management as objectives.","The main objective of fragmentation is to place data closer to the users/applications that frequently access it.\n\n1. Improve query performance\n\nQueries can access only the required fragment instead of the entire table.\n\n2. Reduce communication cost\n\nLess data needs to travel between different network sites.\n\n3. Support parallel processing\n\nDifferent fragments can be processed simultaneously at different sites.\n\n4. Improve availability\n\nData can remain available even if one site becomes temporarily unavailable.\n\n5. Improve scalability\n\nData can be distributed as the database grows.\n\n6. Provide better data management\n\nData can be placed according to geographical location, department, workload, etc.",["16","17"],["9ef9e7ca-8e9b-484f-922c-4b9ad291d932","b6dec40b-1bd4-4a9b-9fd5-4ca9ea472735"],"Fragmentation","medium","conceptual"),
("u3_025","What is Horizontal Fragmentation?","In horizontal fragmentation, a relation is divided based on its rows (tuples). Each fragment contains some rows of the original table and all of its columns.","In horizontal fragmentation, a relation is divided based on its rows (tuples).\n\nEach fragment contains:\n\n- Some rows of the original table\n- All columns of the original table",["17","18"],["b6dec40b-1bd4-4a9b-9fd5-4ca9ea472735","c6266cba-a857-4ec7-b768-09594af586a5"],"Horizontal Fragmentation","medium","definition"),
("u3_026","How does the Employee example demonstrate Horizontal Fragmentation?","The Employee relation is split into location-based row groups: Pune employees (EmpID 1 and 3), Mumbai employees (EmpID 2 and 5), and Delhi employees (EmpID 4). Each fragment retains the original columns.","Suppose employees are divided according to their location.\n\nOriginal relation:\n\nEMPLOYEE\n\nEmpID Dept Location\n\n1 HR Pune\n\n2 IT Mumbai\n\n3 IT Pune\n\n4 HR Delhi\n\n5 Finance Mumbai\n\nWe can create:\n\nFragment F1 - Pune Employees\n\n1 HR Pune\n\n3 IT Pune\n\nFragment F2 - Mumbai Employees\n\n2 IT Mumbai\n\n5 Finance Mumbai\n\nFragment F3 - Delhi Employees\n\n4 HR Delhi",["18"],["c6266cba-a857-4ec7-b768-09594af586a5"],"Horizontal Fragmentation","medium","application"),
("u3_027","What is Replication in a distributed database?","Replication means keeping copies of the same data at multiple locations instead of storing it at only one site.","Replication means keeping copies of the same data at multiple locations.\n\nInstead of storing data at only one site, copies are stored at other sites.",["18"],["c6266cba-a857-4ec7-b768-09594af586a5"],"Replication","easy","definition"),
("u3_028","State one advantage and one disadvantage of Replication from the notes.","An advantage is that data can remain accessible from another location if the original server fails, improving availability and reliability. A disadvantage is that all copies must be updated whenever the original data changes.","Customer Table\n\n- Original → Mumbai\n- Copy → Delhi\n- Copy → Pune\n\nIf Mumbai's server fails, Delhi or Pune can still provide the data.\n\n## Why is it important?\n\n- Faster access.\n- High availability.\n- Better reliability.\n\n## Disadvantage\n\nWhenever the original data changes, all copies must also be updated.",["19"],["7282d32a-97f9-4b52-9a3e-567b6c1fe18f"],"Replication","medium","advantages_disadvantages"),
("u3_029","What does Transparency mean in a distributed database?","Transparency means users do not need to know how or where data is stored; the Distributed DBMS hides internal details such as data location, division, and the existence of copies.","Transparency means users do not need to know how or where the data is stored.\n\nThe Distributed DBMS hides all the internal details from users.\n\nUsers do not know:\n\n- Where the data is stored.\n- How the data is divided.\n- Whether copies exist.",["19"],["7282d32a-97f9-4b52-9a3e-567b6c1fe18f"],"Transparency","easy","definition"),
("u3_030","What is Query Processing in a distributed database?","Query Processing means executing SQL queries efficiently when data is stored at different locations. For a query involving multiple sites, the DBMS decides which server should process it, how data should be transferred, and which execution plan is fastest.","Query Processing means executing SQL queries efficiently when data is stored at different locations.\n\nIf a query needs data from multiple sites, the DBMS decides:\n\n- Which server should process the query?\n- How should data be transferred?\n- Which execution plan is fastest?",["20"],["009bca0b-67c7-4f81-b74c-fed44478c080"],"Query Processing","medium","definition"),
("u3_031","What security measures are listed for a distributed database?","The listed measures are user authentication, password protection, access control, data encryption, and backup and recovery.","Since data is stored across networks, it must be protected.\n\nThe DBMS provides:\n\n- User authentication\n- Password protection\n- Access control\n- Data encryption\n- Backup and recovery",["21"],["57f83ca4-5818-4e45-8f91-e9fce06e9acc"],"Security","easy","list"),
("u3_032","How does the HR salary example illustrate database security?","Only the HR department can access employee salary details; other users cannot view or modify them. This illustrates restricting access to confidential information.","Only the HR department can access employee salary details.\n\nOther users cannot view or modify them.",["21"],["57f83ca4-5818-4e45-8f91-e9fce06e9acc"],"Security","medium","application"),
("u3_033","What is Vertical Fragmentation, according to the supplied chunk?","The supplied chunk states that Vertical Fragmentation divides columns among sites. It does not provide the complete explanation or an example in this chunk.","## 2. Vertical Fragmentation\n\nColumns are divided among sites.",["23"],["10285bc6-013c-4d0e-b069-4cab46e79a99"],"Vertical Fragmentation","easy","definition"),
("u3_034","What is the purpose of the SQL condition shown for Horizontal Fragmentation?","The condition `SELECT * FROM Employee WHERE City='Mumbai';` selects the Employee rows whose City value is Mumbai, illustrating a location-based horizontal fragment.","## Horizontal Fragmentation Rule\n\nSELECT * FROM Employee WHERE City='Mumbai';\n\n## Advantages\n\n- Local processing\n- Reduced network traffic",["23"],["10285bc6-013c-4d0e-b069-4cab46e79a99"],"Horizontal Fragmentation","medium","application"),
("u3_035","What are the listed advantages of Query Processing in a distributed database?","The listed advantages are faster query execution, reduced network traffic, and lower execution cost.","## Why is it important?\n\n- Faster query execution.\n- Reduced network traffic.\n- Lower execution cost.",["20"],["009bca0b-67c7-4f81-b74c-fed44478c080"],"Query Processing","easy","list"),
]

